        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Every day, millions of night workers and air travelers are required to change the timing of their biological clock (circadian system) so that it is correctly aligned to their new work/rest schedule. This very often causes distress related to sleep disruption and to impairments of mood, alertness and performance while awake. Such distress may in some cases lead to accidents and illness. Very often such schedule changes are difficult to make abruptly, and often it is more reasonable to adopt a more gradual approach, trickling in the time changes little by little, rather than in one acute shift in schedule. While the present task is mostly concerned with work/rest scheduling in space, it also addresses an important question for all human beings trying to phase shift their schedule, namely that of whether trickling in a change in routine by repeated phase advances or by repeated phase delays is the better way to do this. Thus earth benefits will accrue to shift workers and air travelers from this task. The knowledge about the response of humans to repeated small phase shifts derived from this research may lead to alternative strategies to ameliorate these problems.
<p>
The aim of this study was to help understand the nature of repeated phase shifts on the human Endogenous Circadian Pacemaker (ECP), and the effects of such repeated shifts on circadian rhythms, sleep, performance, alertness and mood. This will help inform schedulers on future Space Shuttle and International Space Station (ISS) missions. For any phase shift there are two ways of getting to the desired phase position: 1) Clockwise around the clock (repeated phase delays to later bedtimes), or 2) Anti-clockwise against the clock (repeated phase advances to earlier bedtimes). Thus, for example a 6 hour phase advance and an 18 hour phase delay both end up at the same phase position. Crew scheduling guidelines (Appendix K) allow repeated phase advances of up to 30 minutes and repeated phase delays of up to two hours. Investigators hypothesized that it might be better, in some case, to accommodate a given phase advance (e.g., a 6 hour phase advance required for operational reasons), by giving repeated 30 minute phase advances in schedule (to earlier times), than by giving repeated 2 hour phase delays (to later times). The experiment comprised a three year ground-based study of how human sleep, circadian rhythms and performance are affected by repeated phase shifts that are within NASA crew scheduling constraints. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Using time isolation facilities at the University of Pittsburgh investigators simulated two different 16-day missions. Following Appendix K guidelines and standard practice, all sleep periods (subjective "nights") were exactly 8 hours in length. Both missions started with a four-day segment which served as a control comparison, and involved no phase shifts, with subjects having a 2300 bedtime and an 0700 wake time. The "delay" mission required repeated 2 hour phase delays on each of the next nine successive nights of the study (i.e., bedtimes at 0100, 0300, 0500, 0700, etc.), resulting in an eventual movement of bedtimes to an 18 hour phase delayed position, with a bedtime at 1700 and wake time at 0100, which was retained for the remaining nights. The experiment lasted a total of 16 calendar days (384 hours). The "advance" mission started with four control days, after which the protocol required repeated 30 minute phase advances on each successive night of the study (i.e., bedtimes at 2230, 2200, 2130, 2100 etc.). This lead, at the completion of 12 subjective nights, to a 6 hour phase advanced position (a 1700 bedtime, 0100 wake time). Again, the experiment lasted a total of 16 calendar days (384 hours). On all days in both missions, breakfast was 1.5 hours after wake time, lunch 6 hours after wake time, and dinner 12 hours after wake time. Snacks were available ad-lib. Showers and exercise on an exercycle (optional) was restricted to a 1.5h period prior to lunch. Mood, alertness and performance tests were scheduled before each meal, after lunch and before bedtime. Objective measures of sleepiness were given after lunch.
<p>
Healthy volunteers (ages 20 - 59, both genders) lived and worked in the facility singly or in groups of between two and four, and were studied in terms of their sleep (measured polysomnographically and by diary); circadian rhythms (rectal temperature, urinary free cortisol, urinary melatonin sulfate); performance speed and accuracy (on a computerized battery of tests involving vigilance, serial reaction, response inhibition and verbal reasoning); mood, and both subjective and objective measures of alertness/sleepiness. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
One major lesson learned early on in the manned space program was that constraints needed to be imposed upon the timing of a crew's scheduled sleep episodes. If sleep was scheduled at the "wrong" circadian time, then it was likely to be disrupted and unrefreshing. As a means of avoiding sleep disruption problems, NASA originally developed a document referred to as Appendix K which was used to specify the constraints on crew timeline scheduling. Among other goals relating to the need to avoid overburdening the crew, Appendix K sought both to ensure that enough sleep opportunity was given (8 hour per subjective "night"), and to ensure that the placement of the sleep did not violate certain circadian principles which related to maximum amounts by which an astronaut's bedtime could change from one day to the next. Since then several iterations of these rules have been implemented, but all work under the assumption that by "trickling in" phase shifts a little at a time, the endogenous circadian pacemaker can remain appropriately entrained (i.e. remain in correct temporal alignment to the work/sleep routine), thereby avoiding circadian dysfunction with its associated disruptions in sleep and performance functioning. These principles were based on the notion of a "directional asymmetry" whereby phase delays (to later bedtimes) were held to be less disruptive to astronauts' sleep than phase advances (to earlier bedtimes). In particular, they make the assumption that, unlike repeated equivalent phase advances, repeated phase delays of 2 hour per day are within the limits the endogenous circadian pacemaker and thus not likely to lead to circadian dysfunction. Thus, according to Appendix K guidelines, a 6 hour phase advance is better accomplished by nine repeated 2 hour phase delays, rather than by one abrupt 6 hour phase advance. 
<p>
